


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: hankinson
@title: RISM Online: Delivering digital musicology data at scale
@presenter: hankinson
@abstract: 

With over 1.6 million source records, 2.5 million incipits,
160,000 people, and 26,000 institutions, RISM Online
delivers this data to more than 500,000 visitors each month
in seven languages. As one of the largest open
bibliographic resources for musical sources, RISM Online
increasingly serves not only as a discovery platform, but
also as a foundation for computational and data-driven
musicological research.

This talk will begin with a high-level overview of key
features of RISM Online, including its incipit search
engine, multilingual interfaces, and dedicated searches for
national and institutional collections.

The presentation will then discuss several underlying
architectural features that support interoperability,
extensibility, and long-term sustainability. These include
the integration of authority data, persistent identifiers,
and semantic data models that enable connections between
musical works, sources, people, and institutions across
collections.

Finally, the talk will preview ongoing experimental work on
the development of a public Linked Data platform delivering
more than 300 million RDF triples. This work aims to
provide researchers and institutions with new opportunities
for data reuse, network analysis, and cross-collection
discovery, while also positioning RISM within a broader
ecosystem of open cultural heritage and digital humanities
infrastructures.


@@END: PRESENTATION



